  data: {
    LLM: "In Rang Milanti, pure randomness determines the cards you flip, but 'Endurance' is the key. Since it is a game of collection, you want to be the one who triggers a match when the pile is largest. There is no active choice in flipping, but players can speed up or slow down their flipping rhythm (house rules permitting) to 'Time' their cards against a large pile.\n\nThe '40-Card' Advantage: If playing with a 40-card deck (where 2s through 6s are removed), the density of each suit is slightly higher relative to the deck size. This means matches happen more frequently. Pay attention to which suits have been 'Buried' in other players' collected pilesâ€”if you see a lot of Spades being won by a rival, you know the remaining deck is 'Spade-Light', making a Spade match less likely soon.\n\nSocial Momentum: The psychological aspect of Rang Milanti involves the 'Chain Win'. Because a winner gets another turn, they often hit a 'Hot Streak' where they match their own just-won card's suit. If you see a player on a streak, stay alertâ€”they are effectively thinning the deck's variety, making it easier for you to hit a match once their streak breaks.",
